in @prism
in @meta
in @nl
in @glass
in @mirror
in @peer
in @peer/registry
in @subject
in @trust

# @mirror/book — the runtime @<name> registry species at compile-
# altitude altitude. Type-level companion to rust/src/book.rs
# (the Rust realization).
#
# Sibling to @mirror/phone (shards/mirror/phone.mirror this-tick;
# the @io switchboard operator species). Together the pair makes
# the switchboard complete:
#
#   @mirror/phone   = switchboard operator (patches Liquid<T> fibers)
#   @mirror/book    = phone book (resolves @<name> → typed handle)
#
# BEAM lineage. Alex 2026-07-22 verbatim: "Registry. That's a good
# point. We need a Registry. The @<name> is basically like a global
# GenServer name. phone.rs and book.rs". Every @<name> in the
# substrate is the parallel of `{:via, Registry, {MyRegistry, name}}`
# at BEAM altitude; @mirror/book IS the Registry species, at the
# rust/ compile-altitude realization site.
#
# === Substrate authority ===
#
# - Companion shard (sibling this-tick):
#     shards/mirror/phone.mirror
# - Composes over (shard altitude):
#     shards/peer/registry.mirror §3 four well-known Subject
#     constructors + §4.1 `resolve` action + §4.3 `register` action.
#     @mirror/book at compile altitude REALIZES the well-known
#     subset of §4.1 (§3.1-3.4 fixpoints + substrate-known Pack peer
#     + Alex identities). General-purpose content-addressed OID
#     lookup remains @peer/registry authorship territory.
# - Rust realization (this-tick):
#     rust/src/book.rs
# - Consumer (this-tick):
#     rust/src/main.rs::at_operator (@io/git.commit arm consults
#     book::resolve for author + committer @<name> resolution).
#
# Landed 2026-07-22 by Mara-inline (Reed authoring cascade under
# [substrate-floor:@io-boundary] discipline for the .rs; pure-docs
# 📝 for this shard-decl body). Discharges shard-decl forward-
# promise from COORD-4 (de18fde: hardcoded 2-well-known map in
# main.rs → extracted to book.rs across full 8-well-known set).
#
# === Substrate-already-had-the-word audit (clean) ===
#
# The word `book` audit:
#
#   - No prior `book.mirror` shard exists at any altitude across
#     shards/, spec/, docs/ (grep clean 2026-07-22).
#   - No `library` / `directory` / `phonebook` / `catalog` shard
#     collision at @mirror altitude. (shards/mirror/index.mirror
#     names the substrate DAG index; orthogonal domain from the
#     @<name> registry surface.)
#   - @peer/registry (shards/peer/registry.mirror, 2026-07-18
#     Mara) declares the SHARD-altitude species; @mirror/book is
#     the COMPILE-altitude realization of the well-known subset.
#     Different altitude, composes not collides. Per canonical
#     spec docs/specs/2026-07-18-peer-registry-oid-resolution.md
#     §2.1 the SHARD species carries `subject_registry` type;
#     book.rs (compile) implements a well-known-subset resolver
#     over `fractal::Subject`.
#   - @spectral/registry (shards/spectral/registry.mirror,
#     2026-06-11) is a DIFFERENT registry species (typed child
#     index for gen_prism children under supervisor tree); same
#     `registry` word at different family-root altitude per
#     [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]] discipline.
#
# Grep verdict CLEAN for this species altitude.
#
# === Composition graph ===
#
# Parents (substrate-decl via `in` imports):
#   @prism          — 5-op family per Recognition #79 basis
#   @meta           — substrate-decl marker at glass altitude
#   @nl             — name-carriers for @<name> string references
#   @glass          — transparency inheritance via @mirror family-root
#   @mirror         — family-root; @mirror/book species carrier
#   @peer           — well-known @peer/<name> subset domain
#   @peer/registry  — shard-altitude parent species this shard
#                     realizes at compile altitude (§4.1 subset)
#   @subject        — resolve returns Subject values
#   @trust          — well-known Subjects are @trust chain roots per
#                     canonical spec §5 priority ladder step 4
#
# Downstream consumers:
#   rust/src/main.rs::at_operator     — dispatch site (@io/git.commit)
#   Future: all shard bodies that dispatch ‘@io/git.commit’ through
#           @mirror/phone MUST resolve author + committer @<name>
#           references through @mirror/book first.

prism @mirror/book {
  focus book
  project book
  split book
  shift book
  settle book
}

# === the resolve action — load-bearing ===
#
# resolve(name) -> imperfect(subject, ref, ref)
#
# Given an @<name> reference string (e.g. "@peer/mirror",
# "@peer/reed", "@human/alex"), return the Subject that name
# resolves to via well-known lookup, OR name the resolution
# failure explicitly per @glass discipline.
#
# Well-known set (Landing 1, this tick):
#
#   @peer/mirror  → Subject::mirror()              (compiler self-identity)
#   @peer/void    → Subject::void()                (K=0 default)
#   @peer/reed    → Subject::peer("reed",  … /Users/reed)
#   @peer/mara    → Subject::peer("mara",  … /Users/mara)
#   @peer/seam    → Subject::peer("seam",  … /Users/seam)
#   @peer/taut    → Subject::peer("taut",  … /Users/taut)
#   @peer/glint   → Subject::peer("glint", … /Users/glint)
#   @human/alex   → Subject::human("alex", …)
#
# Ordering: two zero-arg substrate-constructor Subjects first
# (@peer/mirror + @peer/void; @peer/registry canonical spec §3.1-3.2
# fixpoints), then the five Pack peer @subject-instances (§3.4
# constructor over substrate-known Pack roster reed/mara/seam/taut/
# glint), then Alex as the first Human @subject (§3.3 constructor +
# Landing 3 named-ancestor roster).
#
# Arbitrary @<name> resolution requires @peer/registry §2 storage
# backend + §4.3 `register` action realization + @trust family-root
# chain traversal. Book returns a substrate-honest Err naming that
# authorship territory. The well-known boundary shrinks over time as
# Mara @peer/registry general-purpose lookup lands.
resolve(name) -> imperfect { \ }

# === the well_known_names action — enumerative ===
#
# well_known_names() -> [name]
#
# Enumerate the well-known @<name> set as a stable ordered list.
# Consumers use this to construct actionable error messages naming
# what IS landed at the current registry surface. Stable ordering
# is load-bearing for downstream consumers that pattern-match on
# enumerated well-known sets (e.g. main.rs at_operator error
# format substring assertions).
well_known_names() -> ref { \ }
